abstract:
Solutions $(u, v)$ to the chemotaxis system  egin{align*}  egin{cases} u_t = 
abla \cdot ( (u+1)^{m-1} 
abla u - u (u+1)^{q-1} 
abla v), \ τv_t = Δv - v + u \end{cases} \end{align*} in a ball $Ω\subset \mathbb R^n$, $n \ge 2$, wherein $m, q \in \mathbb R$ and $τ\in \{0, 1\}$ are given parameters with $m - q > -1$, cannot blow up in finite time provided $u$ is uniformly-in-time bounded in $L^p(Ω)$ for some $p > p_0 :=  rac n2 (1 - (m - q))$. For radially symmetric solutions, we show that, if $u$ is only bounded in $L^{p_0}(Ω)$ and the technical condition $m >  rac{n-2 p_0}{n}$ is fulfilled, then, for any $α>  rac{n}{p_0}$, there is $C > 0$ with  egin{align*} u(x, t) \leq C |x|^{-α} \qquad 	ext{for all $x \in Ω$ and $t \in (0, T_{\max})$}, \end{align*} $T_{\max} \in (0, \infty]$ denoting the maximal existence time. This is essentially optimal in the sense that, if this estimate held for any $α<  rac{n}{p_0}$, then $u$ would already be bounded in $L^{p}(Ω)$ for some $p > p_0$. Moreover, we also give certain upper estimates for chemotaxis systems with nonlinear signal production, even without any additional boundedness assumptions on $u$. The proof is mainly based on deriving pointwise gradient estimates for solutions of the Poisson or heat equation with a source term uniformly-in-time bounded in $L^{p_0}(Ω)$.
